# Mutation plan for the four read routes of `api4/properties.go`.
#
#   scripts/mutate-batch.sh scripts/mutations/properties-routes.plan
#
# Unlike its sibling `cpa-routes.plan`, most of what is worth mutating here is a **query
# predicate** rather than an error id: the `boards` group carries no licence hook, so the parity
# fixture holds seven field rows and three value rows and the suite compares the rows themselves.
# The fixture is built so `create_at` order, `update_at` order and `id` order are three different
# orders — without that, half of the store lines below could be swapped for one another and every
# assertion would still pass.
#
# Two things on this family are invisible to any client and are **not** run:
#
#   * i18n is not ported ([D-092]), so `message` is the raw id and the parity helper excludes it.
#   * `AppError`'s parameter map carries `json:"-"`, so *which* parameter a 400 named, and which
#     permission a 403 named, are not on the wire.
#
# `IncludeDeleted` is unreachable from all four routes — no handler sets it — so the only way a
# tombstone reaches a client is delta mode's auto-include, which is what the two
# `delta-mode-hides-the-tombstones` lines test.
#
# # What the first (aborted) run found, and what was done about it
#
# * `store-fields-always-order-by-create-at` **survived**. The fixture's `update_at` values
#   ascended in the same sequence as its `create_at` values, so delta mode and directory mode
#   returned the same order and the mode switch was unobservable. Two timestamps were swapped;
#   the mutation is caught now. This is the single most important line in this file: without it
#   the whole `CASE WHEN $1` in the field query is untested.
# * `store-values-the-target-filter-is-dropped` **survived**. Every planted value sat on the one
#   channel the tests asked about, so a query with no `TargetID` predicate returned the same rows.
#   Two values were added — one on a DM, one at the system target — and
#   `each_value_route_reads_only_its_own_target` reads all three targets.
# * `store-fields-a-dm-gets-the-team-arm-anyway` was an **equivalent mutant**, not a fixture gap:
#   a DM's `opts.TeamID` is empty, so the arm it added (`targettype = 'team' AND targetid = ''`)
#   can never match a row Go would have written. It is replaced by
#   `store-fields-a-dm-sees-every-team-field`, which drops the target comparison instead.
# * Two lines were **harness faults** rather than verdicts: `$12 <> $12` and `LIMIT $16 + 1000`
#   leave Postgres with no type for the parameter, so the crate did not compile. Both carry an
#   explicit `::bigint` now. A mutation that does not build is not a survivor and is not a catch.
#
# # The valid run: **44 run, 41 caught, 2 controls survived, 0 harness faults** (2026-09-12)
#
# One real survivor, and it was a fixture gap rather than an equivalent mutant:
# `api-the-value-route-serves-the-template-object-type` swaps the handler's `template` guard for
# `user`, and the template refusal **still came back** — from `hasTargetAccess`'s own template arm,
# one layer down, with the same id and the same status. The suite had no `user/values` probe at
# all and only ever asked for `template/values` with a well-formed id in a group that exists.
# Three probes close it (`user/values` is a 200 `null`; `template/values/notanid` and
# `nosuchgroup/template/values/…` are still the template 400, because the guard runs before both
# `RequireTargetId` and the group read), and the mutation is **CAUGHT** on the re-run, by two
# tests. The other 43 verdicts stand unchanged: the fix only adds probes, and an added probe can
# turn a SURVIVED into a CAUGHT but never the reverse.
store-fields-order-by-the-wrong-column	crates/mm-store/src/property_store.rs	ORDER BY (CASE WHEN $1 THEN updateat ELSE createat END) ASC, id ASC\n             LIMIT $16	ORDER BY (CASE WHEN $1 THEN createat ELSE updateat END) ASC, id ASC\n             LIMIT $16	api	properties
